the countdown is almost over after years  of planning the stage is set for the  commonwealth games in birmingham  world-class facilities already  benefiting the city's young talented  athletes  it means everything to have the games  right on my doorstep see all the  athletes from all around the world  coming just to where me and going to  compete  organizers want these games to bring  communities together  recently retired champion triple jumper  nathan douglas spent most of his career  based in the city it's a very  diverse and multicultural city probably  you know up there as the most so in the  country really so for me what i hope is  it's going to inspire local people to  move you know to help them with their  health and well-being physically  mentally and so for me that's what i  kind of look at hopefully is the legacy  of the game  but the backdrop to these games is a  cost of living crisis  there's no doubt the facilities for the  games will be state of the art but with  millions of pounds of public money being  spent there are people in birmingham  asking what's in it for me  in the shadow of the stadium a  community's really struggling  this boxing club has been fighting for  funding but so far received nothing  they have children here whose parents  can no longer afford the two pound  lesson fee but they let them stay for  free if we wasn't there i think after  the kids  would be out on the streets ran the  chips up hanging around with the mates  but off to no good romantic money in the  council spending and to me which on  things that they don't need to do how  about  going around to a few smaller clubs  football clubs swimming clubs boxing  clubs going to see what they're doing  keeping the kids off the street etc and  making donations from the clubs  but organizers insist communities are  benefiting with hundreds of new homes  and the creation of jobs all part of the  plan the city will see well in excess of  a billion pounds invested into  birmingham as a result of hosting the  games i absolutely believe that the  people of birmingham will look back on  the commonwealth games and say that was  the moment this city changed for the  better  but doubts remain over whether that  change will be felt by everyone in the  parts of the city most in need of help  becky johnson sky news birmingham
 

